### [03:20:00 UTC] Step 0 (extended) — research bkraad47/fat_llama_fftw for portable optimizations
Input: DIRECTIVES (verbatim): "lets look at optimizations from the fftw https://github.com/bkraad47/fat_llama_fftw version and apply them here were aiming to release version 2"
Action: Used `gh api` (read-only, public repo) to pull fat_llama_fftw's CHANGELOG.md, setup.py, feed.py, test_feed.py, CURRENT_STATE.md into the scratchpad directory (outside this repo's write scope) and read them in full. This is groundwork, not a generate-code write: generate-code has no WebFetch/network tool, so the coordinator must extract concrete, CUDA-portable findings up front and hand them over as reference material alongside DIRECTIVES (kept separate and unmodified from DIRECTIVES itself, so DIRECTIVES doesn't drift per the DIRECTIVES-handling rule).
Reasoning: fat_llama_fftw is the CPU/pyfftw sibling package, built and iterated by the same skill framework (its own .claude/ rules mirror this repo's), so its CHANGELOG documents a real, independently-verified history of algorithmic fixes to the *same* upscale() pipeline. Per project-mission.md, only DSP/algorithmic ideas are portable here — anything CPU-thread-specific (pyfftw `threads=` multi-threading) or a CPU-only dependency swap must be re-evaluated for the CUDA/CuPy-only path, not copied verbatim.
Output: FFTW_OPTIMIZATION_FINDINGS (frozen reference, to be passed unmodified alongside DIRECTIVES into every generate-code dispatch this run):
  1. **Peak-relative IST threshold.** fat_llama's own iterative_soft_thresholding docstring already flags this as a known, unfixed issue (cycle 4: threshold_value is compared against raw magnitudes, not scaled to the signal's own peak, so IST barely sparsifies real audio). fftw fixed this: initialize_ist masks `abs(data) > threshold * peak(data)`; each iteration's FFT-domain mask uses `abs(fft) > threshold * peak(fft)`. Also always excludes the DC bin (index 0) from the FFT-domain mask (asymmetric transients can otherwise make DC the single loudest bin, injecting a DC offset/drone).
  2. **IST convergence early-exit.** fftw's _ist_chain stops the max_iter loop early once a pass changes the estimate by less than a relative tolerance (1e-6 of the chain's own initial scale) — hard-threshold IST is a fixed-point projection, so later iterations after convergence are provably wasted compute. Directly portable to the CUDA loop.
  3. **Block/WOLA local IST instead of one whole-buffer FFT per iteration.** fftw's biggest quality fix: thresholding the *whole file* against one global peak lets the single loudest moment dominate the cutoff, leaving quieter passages/other bands with no measurable added detail (this is likely why fat_llama's own coherence notes "safe, transparent... rather than clearly measurable added detail"). fftw processes signals longer than block_size=8192 as 50%-overlap sqrt-Hann-windowed blocks (STFT-style), runs the (converging) IST chain per block, then reconstructs via weighted overlap-add (WOLA, windowed on both analysis and synthesis side to avoid block-boundary clicks) with a window-shaped (not flat-scalar) DC-leak correction per block. This is the change most likely to produce genuinely measurable added detail rather than a transparent pass-through, but is also the riskiest/most involved to port correctly (multiple sub-bugs were found and fixed across fftw's own cycles 2-5 for this exact feature: DC dominance, un-tapered block edges/clicking, flat-scalar DC correction re-opening the click).
  4. **Cap IST's contribution to the pre-IST baseline peak.** fftw's _cap_ist_changes_to_baseline_peak rescales (bounded, max_rounds=20 iterative shrink) the combined interpolated+IST signal so its peak never exceeds the plain-interpolation baseline's own peak — otherwise IST's own peak growth gets "spent" out of every frequency (not just the ones IST boosted) at the final normalize, causing measured net attenuation (up to ~1.3dB) in bands IST never touched.
  5. Channel-level concurrency (ThreadPoolExecutor over stereo channels) and multi-threaded pyfftw for large-signal Nyquist cutoff are CPU/GIL-specific — NOT directly portable to CUDA/CuPy (cp.fft is already GPU-parallel internally); flagged as out of scope for a direct port, per project-mission.md's CUDA-only constraint. If channel throughput is worth revisiting, that would mean investigating CUDA streams, a distinct CUDA-native investigation, not a copy of this technique.
  6. read_audio's pydub->soundfile migration (simpler deps, no ffmpeg subprocess, correct N-channel shape instead of a hardcoded 2-channel reshape) is a plausible but riskier port (MP3-via-soundfile depends on libsndfile version/build); flagged as a candidate for generate-code to evaluate empirically against this repo's actual test fixtures, not a mandatory change.
  7. fftw's own compute_upscale_factor has NO upper clamp (confirmed via its own 1.4.5 changelog entry) — fat_llama's version is already ahead here (MAX_REALISTIC_SAMPLE_RATE_HZ clamp); nothing to port for this piece.
  8. Testing: fftw added a real unmocked end-to-end FLAC-write test and a decimate-back-and-correlate source/output coherence test in its own test suite. Worth flagging as a possible test-coverage gap to check for in this repo's fat_llama/tests, alongside the algorithmic ports above.

